WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.860
The 2,400-kilometer-long Himalayas

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.690
include the highest peaks,
beautiful landscapes

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.493
and some of the most
contentious areas in the world.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.780
The mountainous region
separates two rising superpowers

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:20.110
that contain over a third
of the planet's population.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.473
And there's a big problem.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.420
It's not well-demarcated.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.290
There are not signposts everywhere.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.150
It's not even very well-populated.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.530
Very few people live up there.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:33.220
It's very tricky to have a
sense of who's controlling

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.690
what bits of that very long border.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:39.310
And it's very easy for
accidents to happen.

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:40.470
Boundary disagreements

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.280
led to a deadly military clash

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.133
between the two sides for
the first time since 1975.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.350
Adding to the border disputes

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.880
is China's Belt and Road Initiative,

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.020
a massive infrastructure project aimed

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.130
at spreading Beijing's
influence all over the globe,

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:59.773
including countries which
are at odds with India.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.750
We know that Pakistan is a big part

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.470
of China's Belt and Road Initiative.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:08.260
We know that China has
been investing in ports.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.490
They've been investing
in roads in Pakistan.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:11.780
And India and Pakistan

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.433
do share a very contentious
relationship also.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.420
In response to all of this,

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.110
India has increased
military on the border,

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:21.600
banned Chinese apps

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.850
and improved ties with strategic partners

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.400
to try and prevent the
spread of Chinese influence.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.360
India believes that China
wants a unipolar Asia

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.640
that is dominated by China,

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.850
where China sets the rules of the road

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.053
and where countries acquiesce
to Chinese needs and wants.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.290
While a war may be unlikely,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.880
China has made it known it aims

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.463
to take back what it
sees as its territory.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.670
Xi Jinping has made it
his goal to put China

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.510
at what he says is the
center stage of the world.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:56.670
And he's also said

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.700
he wants China to stand tall in the world.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.730
I think with that sort of rhetoric,

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:03.170
it leaves very little
room for China to be able

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.613
to maneuver and give concessions.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.110
The late 1940s saw China's
Communists overthrow

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.970
the ruling government to
form what it is today,

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:21.290
the People's Republic of China.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:24.790
The hero of the revolution was Mao Zedong.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.100
That same decade, India
gained independence

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:27.933
from Great Britain

00:02:27.933 --> 00:02:30.513
to create their current
democratic government.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.050
India was one of the first countries

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.990
to recognize the People's
Republic of China.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:39.500
And they did have the
first few years where India

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.070
and China really did
partner with each other

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:43.860
and saw each other as countries

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.580
that could move forward together.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.790
There was a little bit of an
Asia for Asians, or India-China

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:53.450
brothers' kind of atmosphere
or at least friends.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.400
But that didn't last long.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.840
Shortly after establishing the
People's Republic of China,

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.950
the government exerted
its control over Tibet.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:06.610
During the revolution in which
the Chinese Communist Party

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.080
took control of the country,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.930
in the late '50s, they
then sent troops into Tibet

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:12.280
to take control of that region,

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.590
which had been semi-autonomous,

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.470
semi-independent up until that point.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:20.340
As a result of that,
the Dalai Lama in 1959

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.210
fled to India,

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:22.810
took refuge in India.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.110
The Dalai Lama declared

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.310
that his escape would
not have been possible

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.970
without the loyal support of his people.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:30.550
Although they'd signed an agreement

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:31.630
meant for both sides

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:34.670
to respect each other's
territorial integrity,

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.833
India and China began to confront
each other on the border.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:42.950
China became convinced then that not only

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:46.540
was India at fault for
accepting the Dalai Lama

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:47.790
and these refugees,

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:51.020
but also accused India of
actually colluding with the U.S.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.280
and the U.K. to engineer his escape.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.030
And so by the early '60s,

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:57.840
you really saw a relationship

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.800
where there was very
little trust and a sense

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.310
that the agreement had
essentially kind of broken down,

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.000
or as far as India was concerned,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.263
was not being respected by Beijing.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.480
The first skirmishes took place

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.900
when India discovered China
had been building roads

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.570
through what it viewed as its territory.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:23.180
And just a few years later,

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.330
the most significant
border conflict occurred.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.240
The point at which India
and China sort of disputes

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.960
really reached their climax was in 1962

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:35.010
when the two sides fought a
war over the same territory

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:37.523
that they're fighting over
now up in the Himalayas.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.670
Thousands died on both sides,

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:43.580
with China reaching its goal
of retaining a large area

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:46.993
of land near northwestern
India called Aksai Chin.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.480
Since then, there have
been numerous conflicts

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.553
along what's now the longest
disputed border on the planet.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:58.510
The areas of contention: the
northwestern Himalayan region

00:04:58.510 --> 00:04:59.820
known as Ladakh;

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.350
the Doklam plateau, a territory

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:06.350
that intersects China, Bhutan and India;

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.030
and Arunachal Pradesh,

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:10.750
the eastern territory in
India that China claims

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.263
is part of southern Tibet.

00:05:13.819 --> 00:05:15.240
There have been borders drawn up

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.400
by various British
cartographers in the past.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.824
There's the Ardagh-Johnson
Line marking the border

00:05:20.824 --> 00:05:22.150
in Ladakh;

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:23.730
the Macartney-MacDonald Line,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.186
another proposed marking in Ladakh;

00:05:26.186 --> 00:05:28.790
and McMahon Line, which marks the border

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:30.283
of Arunachal Pradesh.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.927
We have a definite cartographic vision

00:05:34.927 --> 00:05:37.069
about our boundaries based

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:39.430
on what the British has thought about.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:42.440
It's based on a little bit of geography,

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.510
a little bit of ethnicity,

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.470
a little bit of history.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:47.560
So it's very well-defined boundaries,

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.523
which has not been recognized by China.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:52.900
Although some British-drawn
lines were agreed upon

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:54.800
by past governments in Tibet,

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:57.253
they were never formally
accepted by Beijing.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.030
And this is precisely where the clash is

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:04.770
because there is no
second line at the moment

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:06.790
where both agree,

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:09.160
especially in these
disputed areas in the north

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:13.153
and the east where the actual border lies.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.400
But there have been many periods

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:20.783
since the 1962 war when
there wasn't conflict.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:27.270
What they did between 1993 and 2012, 2013,

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.860
in the two-decade period,

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.470
they set up a series of agreements,

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:31.900
which essentially established

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:33.410
that a lot of those territories

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.100
that both countries claim

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.430
would be both man's land, so to speak.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.500
Neither side would disturb the status quo,

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.750
both sides would get
to patrol those regions

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:48.400
and both sides would not
establish a permanent presence

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:49.233
in the region.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:51.240
This solution is known

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.400
as the Line of Actual Control,

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:54.740
and it's a temporary border

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:57.640
where both sides maintain the status quo.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.793
But even that line's location
isn't fully agreed upon.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.890
In 2013, a dispute occurred

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.350
in the Demchok valley near Ladakh,

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:09.760
and another in 2015 where
Indian soldiers destroyed

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.140
a Chinese-built structure

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.770
in what they saw as their territory.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.000
Then things really started

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.750
to heat up near the Doklam plateau.

00:07:18.046 --> 00:07:22.030
There was one boundary crisis in 2017,

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:24.590
where it actually involved
the boundary dispute

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:26.686
between China and Bhutan,

00:07:26.686 --> 00:07:29.160
which again, it's not quite demarcated,

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.070
but there's an understanding about it.

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:32.520
India, as an ally of Bhutan,

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:36.150
got involved to help
push China back or get it

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:39.150
to reverse its movement
or its unilateral change

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.453
of the status quo there.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:43.600
China brought heavy
road-building equipment

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:47.480
into the disputed region and
began constructing a road,

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:49.470
one that would give China a direct route

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.470
to the Siliguri Corridor,
or chicken's neck,

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:55.880
where all land-based military
and commercial traffic travel

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.009
between India's northeastern
provinces and the rest

00:07:58.009 --> 00:07:59.293
of the country.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:02.870
So if there ever is a war

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:05.945
or a major conflict
between China and India,

00:08:05.945 --> 00:08:08.883
this would be strategically
significant land.

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:11.390
Bhutan allowed India's military

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:14.340
to enter their country
to stop the construction.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:16.250
For months the troops faced off,

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:18.193
but no casualties were reported.

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:27.270
After that incident,

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:28.470
India and China appeared

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.070
to have put their differences behind them.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.610
China's relationship with India,

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:36.040
that had sort of looked promising.

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:40.210
As recently as 2019, Xi
Jinping traveled to India,

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:42.070
had this two-day summit with Modi,

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:44.650
and just before that, Modi came to China

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:47.640
and had a multi-day summit
with Xi Jinping here.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.740
The two countries were sort
of finding a common ground

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:52.190
where they can work together.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.710
India and China had actually
established economic ties

00:08:56.710 --> 00:09:00.700
that were perhaps at their
greatest point in history.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:03.850
China had become India's
largest trading partner.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:07.010
But you also saw Chinese
investment for the first time

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:08.130
going into India

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:12.290
in a significant way, and
China was seeking to enter

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:13.450
in an even bigger way,

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:16.610
particularly in India's
telecommunication sector

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:17.783
and the 5G network.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:22.430
Then in 2020, the pandemic happened.

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:25.870
The breaking news is about
the confirmed coronavirus

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:26.790
in Kerala,

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:27.870
where a student studying

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.470
in China's Wuhan University
has tested positive

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:31.840
with the deadly virus...

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:39.360
Because of China's handling of Covid,

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:44.150
India started becoming concerned
about its overdependence

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:46.250
on China in certain sectors,

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:50.100
the lack of transparency of
certain Chinese companies,

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:51.865
their links with the state

00:09:51.865 --> 00:09:56.750
and whether the Chinese state
would use its economic ties

00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:00.723
for whether it was coercion
or economic influence.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.160
Up until this point,

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.440
most of the standoffs had been non-violent

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.420
because of agreements between both sides

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.724
not to use firearms.

00:10:10.724 --> 00:10:14.260
The majority of the confrontations
during the 20th century

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:17.010
used loudspeakers to taunt
each other from far away.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:19.330
Chinese troops have set up loudspeakers

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:22.610
that belt out Punjabi numbers
at their forward posts.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:23.510
Let's take a look.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:31.730
But soon after the pandemic broke out,

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:33.883
the border confrontations turned deadly.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:39.840
In May 2020, China surprised
India by deploying troops

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:41.990
in the disputed region of Ladakh

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.963
and Pakistani-occupied Kashmir.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:47.882
China says India's recent
construction of roads,

00:10:47.882 --> 00:10:50.530
tunnels and the refurbishment of airfields

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:53.910
in the area along the border
changed the status quo

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.593
and forced China to respond.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.000
After weeks of skirmishes,

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:14.660
20 Indian soldiers and at least
four Chinese soldiers died

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:16.850
in hand-to-hand combat.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:19.290
And it came at a time
when economic relations

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:21.673
between the two countries
were at its peak.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:26.810
This is a question that
continues to baffle

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:30.180
a number of experts who focus
both on China and India.

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:35.180
Why would Beijing essentially
put at stake a relationship

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.970
with India that has been
growing in importance

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:42.380
and that China has in part pursued

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:44.580
to keep a country like India away

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:46.990
from its main adversary, the U.S.?

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:49.570
Why would China put this at risk?

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:54.540
And the answers that we have
had are kind of twofold:

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:56.220
One, they believed

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:00.300
that this was a tactically
significant piece of territory,

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:03.810
and that they did not anticipate
the backlash from India.

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:06.990
They thought like in other parts of Asia

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:09.810
that they would essentially
establish their claims,

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:10.740
then India would accept that

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:13.533
and that they would go
back to business as usual.

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:19.540
Although the confrontation ended,

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:21.263
building along the border didn't.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:23.940
In early 2021,

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.900
China completed the construction
of a new village high

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:30.430
in the mountains near the
Doklam plateau called Pangda,

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:33.620
the latest addition to a handful
of villages China has built

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:36.320
to fortify the Tibetan borderlands.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:37.940
But there's another problem.

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:39.710
Some of these villages are located

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:41.773
inside land claimed by Bhutan.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.123
It's the classic

00:12:44.123 --> 00:12:46.700
possession is nine-tenths
of the law phenomena.

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:48.400
It is China saying,

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:50.742
we're just going to change
facts on the ground,

00:12:50.742 --> 00:12:52.360
establish a presence.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:55.463
And the idea has been that
nobody will push back.

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:58.500
Experts refer to their tactic

00:12:58.500 --> 00:12:59.950
as salami slicing,

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:01.710
and it reflects the wider strategy

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:03.610
of China's leader Xi Jinping

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:07.170
to assert the country's
territorial claims in small steps,

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:08.823
while not addressing it head on.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:13.240
China's overall strategy
for a lot of things,

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:16.870
and I'm extrapolating at this
point to include the border,

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:21.480
is to try and push questions
that cannot be resolved today

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:22.770
into the future.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:25.750
And the thought behind
that is that any issue

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:27.690
that China has to tackle today,

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:30.730
if it can tackle it in five,
10 years down the road,

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:33.473
China will be in a stronger
position at that point.

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:37.950
China's end goal is unknown.

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:40.120
But considering the sparsely populated,

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:42.630
inaccessible areas along the border,

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:45.600
the reasons for claiming new
territory seem to be less

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.410
about wanting the land for economic

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:49.563
or strategic, military reasons.

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:53.280
There are other explanations,

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:56.950
of which the primary
one is that Xi Jinping

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:58.870
and the Communist Party of China,

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:02.010
wants to prove to their own public,

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:04.750
that even though they had
made some missteps at home

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:06.260
in terms of handling Covid,

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.010
they could help take
over these territories

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.880
or establish their presence in
territories that China claims

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:14.100
on their sovereignty grounds.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:18.490
And so essentially this
was a nationalism argument.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:21.500
China's obviously become
much more assertive

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:22.950
in its territorial claims.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:24.000
I think a lot of people have talked

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:25.737
about this Wolf Warrior diplomacy

00:14:25.737 --> 00:14:29.630
where Chinese diplomats
are much more aggressive

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:31.910
in verbally defending,
rhetorically defending

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:32.880
China's position.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:36.710
China's obviously backed that
up with more military spending

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:39.810
but you've also seen it
reflected in Chinese state media.

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:41.180
They go to greater lengths

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:44.870
to celebrate Chinese
military accomplishments.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:46.850
They go to greater lengths to show

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:49.010
that Chinese territorial claims

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:50.623
are being upheld and defended.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:53.690
In the South China Sea,

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:56.190
few are willing to fight
back China's claims.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:57.623
But India is different.

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:00.820
The problems in the South China Sea,

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:03.960
including a number of different
claimants who are smaller

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:07.640
or perhaps not willing
or able to take China on,

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:11.090
those don't relate to
the China-India situation

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:15.303
where India has the
ability to hold its ground.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:19.580
Although India lags behind China

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:20.810
in terms of military,

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:22.960
technology and infrastructure,

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:25.200
it may be one of the few countries willing

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:27.113
to contest China in the region.

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:33.180
India has already opposed
China's Belt and Road Initiative,

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:36.630
which some estimate involves
as much as $8 trillion

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:39.623
aimed at creating economic
ties all over the globe.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:44.490
It was one of the first
major countries to announce

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:47.270
that it was going to boycott
the first Belt and Road Forum.

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:50.210
And it does not do so because it says

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:52.530
that it does not respect standards

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:56.027
like financial sustainability,
environmental sustainability,

00:15:56.027 --> 00:15:59.250
transparency and territorial integrity.

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:01.273
That last bit is important for India

00:16:01.273 --> 00:16:04.320
and one of the key reasons
it does not subscribe

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.190
to the Belt and Road Initiative.

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:07.980
India believes that China,

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:12.388
through its China-Pakistan
Economic Corridor,

00:16:12.388 --> 00:16:15.430
in what India considers disputed territory

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:16.900
between India and Pakistan.

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:18.270
It's likely that India will continue

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:22.323
to boycott the Belt and Road
Initiative for the near future.

00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:27.280
China's handling of
Covid, the border crisis

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:29.350
and the Belt and Road have all led India

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:31.510
to look elsewhere for economic ties,

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:34.193
determined to become
less reliant on China.

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:37.680
After the 2020 flareups,

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:40.110
India banned dozens of Chinese apps,

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:42.960
and Prime Minister Modi
doubled down on the Quad,

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.460
an association of four democracies,

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:48.710
Australia, Japan, India and the U.S.,

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:50.910
committed to preventing the Indo-Pacific

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:53.890
from becoming Beijing's
geopolitical domain.

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:56.930
It's cemented a group
of strong democracies

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:59.660
that will work together going forward

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:02.333
to secure a free and open Indo-Pacific.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:05.620
And this is great news for the U.S.

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:08.493
who's looked to increase
ties with India for decades.

00:17:09.610 --> 00:17:11.599
U.S. policy makers have seen India

00:17:11.599 --> 00:17:14.170
as a geopolitical counterbalance

00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:16.040
and economic alternative

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:19.080
and a democratic contrast to China.

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:21.970
Even if it's not an ally and the fact

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:24.430
that it does have a rivalry with China,

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:27.320
that China would have to
dedicate a certain amount

00:17:27.320 --> 00:17:29.774
of its own resources

00:17:29.774 --> 00:17:33.190
in defending against India
or in tackling India.

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:36.170
And so that in and of itself could help

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:38.000
with the balance of power

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:41.550
in the Indo-Pacific. And
therefore they have also talked

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:45.330
about not just approving of India's rise,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.080
but it is in U.S.
national security interest

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:50.283
to support India's rise.

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:52.670
If you look at India,

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:56.140
the pace of growth, the
size of the population,

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:57.240
it's a huge market.

00:17:57.240 --> 00:18:00.840
You have Chinese companies
like TikTok, Huawei,

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:03.540
all of which thought they were
going to have big businesses

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:06.390
in India, and that's obviously
changed in the recent past.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:09.140
Although India has a history

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:11.800
of non-alignment dating
back from the Cold War,

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:13.647
the intense standoff in recent years

00:18:13.647 --> 00:18:17.130
may have finally pushed
them to choose sides.

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:19.120
The U.S. and India have a lot to gain

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:21.400
if they can hold the line against China,

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:23.303
and much to lose if they can't.

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:29.350
I think the Chinese do see
as the greater issue here

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:32.400
India sort of forming this
alliance with the U.S.,

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:33.370
joining the Quad.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:35.480
I think Beijing is
calibrating its response

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:39.010
to the border dispute to try and to defend

00:18:39.010 --> 00:18:41.890
its territorial claims
but also not to do so much

00:18:41.890 --> 00:18:44.441
that it pushes India into
the American embrace.

00:18:44.441 --> 00:18:48.970
We will work together,
closer than ever before.

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:51.620
The best case scenario
for how this plays out

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:55.360
between India and China is they
resolve the border dispute.

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:59.080
They pull their troops
back. Trade resumes.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:02.460
But I think that that is
also a very unlikely outcome,

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:06.394
given the recent Indian
participation in the Quad,

00:19:06.394 --> 00:19:09.640
given how much effort the U.S.

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:12.250
has put into strengthening
that relationship

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:14.470
and how poorly the relationship

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:16.210
between China and the U.S. is going.

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:19.380
It does seem unlikely that India and China

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:21.473
are going to have happy days anytime soon.

